{"ts": 0.02999, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\u001b[3J\u001b[H\u001b[2J", "len": 11}
{"ts": 0.031382, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\u001b[90m--\u001b[0m \u001b[93mshelltutor\u001b[0m \u001b[90m--\u001b[0m \u001b[90mModule : shells\u001b[0m \u001b[90m--------------------------------------------\u001b[0m \u001b[90m--\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n", "len": 138}
{"ts": 0.031465, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\u001b[1;97mshells: what runs your commands\u001b[0m\r\n\r\nThree short lessons, then a task. This module stands alone and saves\r\nnothing. You'll look at the shell as a program, at other shells, and\r\n", "len": 186}
{"ts": 0.031514, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "at the difference between your login shell and this one.\r\n\r\nAssumes the five stages, or that you know \u001b[92mls\u001b[0m, \u001b[92mcd\u001b[0m and \u001b[92m>\u001b[0m.\r\n\r\n\u001b[93m> Type \u001b[92mnext\u001b[93m for lesson 1.\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n", "len": 194}
{"ts": 0.034768, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\u001b[1;90mnext\u001b[0m \u001b[90m(go on)\u001b[0m   \u001b[1;90mshow\u001b[0m \u001b[90m(this screen again)\u001b[0m   \u001b[1;90mprev\u001b[0m \u001b[90m(back)\u001b[0m   \u001b[1;90mquit\u001b[0m \u001b[90m(save and leave)\u001b[0m\r\n", "len": 159}
{"ts": 0.034981, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\u001b[90m--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\u001b[0m\r\n", "len": 91}
{"ts": 0.034998, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\r\n", "len": 2}
{"ts": 0.047734, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\u001b[?2004hshelltutor> ", "len": 20}
{"ts": 0.047857, "dir": "in", "enc": "utf8", "data": "next\n", "len": 5}
{"ts": 0.047888, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "next\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r", "len": 15}
{"ts": 0.05257, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\u001b[3J\u001b[H\u001b[2J", "len": 11}
{"ts": 0.05379, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\u001b[90m--\u001b[0m \u001b[90mModule\u001b[0m \u001b[90m-------------------------------------------------------\u001b[0m \u001b[93mshells  1/3\u001b[0m \u001b[90m--\u001b[0m\r\n", "len": 127}
{"ts": 0.053836, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\r\n", "len": 2}
{"ts": 0.053893, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\u001b[1;97mThe shell is a program\u001b[0m\r\n\r\nThe shell reads your line, finds the program you named, and runs it.\r\nIt is itself a program, and you can look at it.\r\n", "len": 156}
{"ts": 0.053909, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\r\n  \u001b[92mecho $BASH_VERSION\u001b[0m       \u001b[90m# the version of the shell running now\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[92mps -p $$ -o pid,comm\u001b[0m     \u001b[90m# this shell, as a running program\u001b[0m\r\n\r\nThe second line prints one row: a number and a name. The name ends in\r\nbash. The shell is a program like any other; \u001b[92m$$\u001b[0m is its number.\r\n\r\n\u001b[93m> Run both, then type \u001b[92mnext\u001b[93m.\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n", "len": 366}
{"ts": 0.05713, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\u001b[1;90mnext\u001b[0m \u001b[90m(go on)\u001b[0m   \u001b[1;90mshow\u001b[0m \u001b[90m(this screen again)\u001b[0m   \u001b[1;90mprev\u001b[0m \u001b[90m(back)\u001b[0m   \u001b[1;90mquit\u001b[0m \u001b[90m(save and leave)\u001b[0m\r\n", "len": 159}
{"ts": 0.057338, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\u001b[90m--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n", "len": 93}
{"ts": 0.073115, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\u001b[?2004hshelltutor> ", "len": 20}
